:\"This page could not be found.\"}]}]]}]}]],[]],\"forbidden\":\"$undefined\",\"unauthorized\":\"$undefined\"}]}]]}]}]]}],{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"error\":\"$undefined\",\"errorStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"errorScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{}],\"templateStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"templateScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"notFound\":\"$undefined\",\"forbidden\":\"$undefined\",\"unauthorized\":\"$undefined\"}]]}],{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"min-h-screen bg-[#060606] text-neutral-400 font-light selection:bg-white selection:text-black pb-32\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"header\",null,{\"className\":\"fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-40 p-6 grid grid-cols-3 items-center bg-[#060606]/80 backdrop-blur-sm border-b border-neutral-900/50\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-start\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L4\",null,{\"href\":\"/\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"group relative inline-flex items-center justify-center px-4 py-2 font-sans transition-colors duration-300 text-sm text-neutral-500 hover:text-white\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mr-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"[\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"tracking-widest uppercase\",\"children\":\"< Back\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ml-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"]\"}]]}]}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-center\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm md:text-base font-normal tracking-[0.3em] text-neutral-300 uppercase\",\"children\":\"About\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-end\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L4\",null,{\"href\":\"/photos/uploads/resume_tech_eng.pdf\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"group relative inline-flex items-center justify-center px-4 py-2 font-sans transition-colors duration-300 text-sm text-neutral-500 hover:text-white\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mr-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"[\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"tracking-widest uppercase\",\"children\":\"Resume\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ml-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"]\"}]]}]}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"className\":\"pt-48 px-6 md:px-12 max-w-[1800px] mx-auto\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-32 border-b border-neutral-800 pb-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-4xl md:text-5xl font-light tracking-tight text-neutral-200 mb-6 uppercase\",\"children\":\"Cheerful Nonsense.\"}],\"$L5\",\"$L6\"]}],\"$L7\",\"$L8\"]}]]}],[\"$L9\",\"$La\"],\"$Lb\"]}],{},null,false,false]},null,false,false]},null,false,false],\"$Lc\",false]],\"m\":\"$undefined\",\"G\":[\"$d\",[]],\"S\":true}\nf:I[78588,[\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\"],\"JournalCard\"]\n16:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"OutletBoundary\"]\n17:\"$Sreact.suspense\"\n19:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"ViewportBoundary\"]\n1b:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"MetadataBoundary\"]\n5:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-lg text-neutral-500 font-mono tracking-tighter mb-8\",\"children\":\"> My monologue.\"}]\ne:T5d1,Hi. I'm Zuocheng Wang.  I'm currently in University of Pittsburgh undergraduate program, majoring 'Computer Science' and 'Digital Narrative & Interactive Design'. I’d like to think of myself as someone with a broad range of interests. If you walked up to me and started talking about your hobbies, no matter what they are, I could probably hold my own in the conversation. \n\nI’ve always had a bone to pick with the Chinese public education system’s obsession with sorting people into \"Arts vs. Sciences.\" It creates this false dichotomy, subtly erasing the possibility of being both. To me, art decides the destination, while technology decides how to get there; possessing both is the truly rare skill of our time. This isn't \"distraction\" or \"unproductive.\" My enjoyment is my greatest priority.\n\nAs the headline says, \"The world is a cheerful nonsense\" (Hermann Hesse). There is no singular, noble \"meaning\" out there that demands we sacrifice infinite possibilities in our short lives. Look, the world is getting more beautiful by the second.\n\nThis page was originally intended to be a traditional CV. However, since the cool stuffs I made are already documented in their respective sections, and my formal academic information and technical history can be found in my resume linked in the corner, a standard CV felt redundant here. Instead, I've converted this space into a living archive, a repository for monologues, fragments, and late-night thoughts. Welcome to the journal.6:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"text-neutral-400 text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap\",\"children\":[\"$e\"]}]\n10:T5ca,My mom asked me why I suddenly decided not to start working right after graduation.I told her:\n\n“Why not? I love the feeling of having options.I want to get into Big Tech—the money is great. But then again, maybe not—it’s exhausting, and I’d probably lose my hair to the grind. I want to be a Product Manager; the sense of achievement from building something is huge. But then again, maybe not—dealing with a hundred different personalities and playing the peacemaker sounds like a drag.\n\nI want to make films, maybe join a top-tier production house like MediaStorm. I want to be a creator, like the guys at Speedster. I even want to build rockets from scratch in a backyard workshop.\n\nI want to be a photographer, join a film crew, drive race cars for a factory team, or be a designer. I want to make music—play guitar in a dive bar and sing whatever song I’m obsessed with lately, not giving a damn whether the crowd is listening or not.\n\nI just suddenly realized: if I finish a Master’s degree and just start climbing the corporate ladder, my life is basically set in stone. Sure, I’ll make plenty of money and settle down in a big city, but I’ll lose my choices. The cost of turning back will be too high. Kids need school, and a wife might be shouting about divorce every time she’s unhappy. Good God!\n\nI just want to grab it all while I’m young: the best views, the best girls, the best version of myself, and the best opportunities.”\n\nMy Mom: 😮11:T9e7,\"I miss you. What are you up to?\"\n\nI don't know who to send this to. I’ll just leave it here.\n\nHave I become the person I once despised, living off hollow intimacy? I’ve grown used to having someone—beside me or in my phone. It doesn't really matter who. I just need that silent belief that someone loves me, that someone \"got my back.\" Eventually, I grow tired, annoyed. I watch her cry until her tears run dry, then I replace her with someone new, feeling absolutely nothing. All those blurred lines and ambiguities... perhaps they are just the shadows of \"youth.\" Dark, yet structured, they are the evidence of my existence on the canvas—the inevitable result of being exposed to the sun. Or so I tell myself to ease the guilt.\n\nI used to be so insecure. I witnessed couples blooming all around me; I watched my friends walk away and into the arms of others, leaving me behind. I blessed them from the bottom of my heart, yet I screamed from the depths of it, too. I craved validation so much. I was so desperate for tenderness. I just wanted someone to hold me and tell me, \"Everything is going to be okay.\" To treat me like a child, or an infant. If I could return to the womb, that would be even better. Maybe my mother had her own troubles back then, or perhaps she simply lacked the power to stuff me back in and start over.\n\nIs being liked the only form of self-affirmation? I refuse to believe that. So, I slapped my younger self across the face. He became even more insecure.\n\nFor someone with such fierce pride, I never resisted dressing as a girl. Staring at that stranger in the mirror, I’d sigh—that kind of girl would never love me, but I could briefly become her. But when I take off the wig and the skirt, she doesn’t leave. She made me fall in love with a concept, an image, a perfect \"Me.\" So maybe, there was never room for anyone else in my heart. There is only me, and Me.\n\n\"What are we now?\" I have no answer. Am I afraid of a predetermined future? Promises I can't keep? Or is it that I simply don't love you as much as I love myself? Of course I love you, but I just love everyone equally... right? Or do I just want to be loved by everyone equally, so I can squander it as I please.\n\nThrough all these years of drifting, the only skill I’ve truly mastered is \"not giving a damn.\" I never learned how to meet or embrace; I only learned how to say goodbye. I know I’m pushing a boulder. I know it will eventually roll back down. I know this is my punishment.\n\nTell me, Mr. Camus—am I happy?7:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-16 mb-64\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-02-24-the-future-is-yet-to-come\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-02-24-the-future-is-yet-to-come\",\"title\":\"The future is yet to come\",\"date\":\"2026-02-24T00:03:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"Don't want to limit myself.\",\"content\":\"$10\"}}],[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-02-16-exhausted\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-02-16-exhausted\",\"title\":\"Exhausted\",\"date\":\"2026-02-16T00:00:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"\\\"I miss you. What are you up to?\\\"\",\"content\":\"$11\"}}],\"$L12\",\"$L13\",\"$L14\",\"$L15\"]}]\n8:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto text-left\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-neutral-600 font-mono\",\"children\":[\"//\",\" Thinking in public.\"]}]}]\n9:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-0\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"async\":true,\"nonce\":\"$undefined\"}]\na:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-1\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\",\"async\":true,\"nonce\":\"$undefined\"}]\nb:[\"$\",\"$L16\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$17\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.MetadataOutlet\",\"children\":\"$@18\"}]}]\nc:[\"$\",\"$1\",\"h\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L19\",null,{\"children\":\"$L1a\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"hidden\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L1b\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$17\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.Metadata\",\"children\":\"$L1c\"}]}]}],[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"next-size-adjust\",\"content\":\"\"}]]}]\n1d:T917,I just whipped up a bowl of Buldak noodles,\n\nWhen a friend sent over a photo—\n\nA plate of crawfish we once shared.\n\nWhat defines a \"crawfish\" now?\n\nI’ve long lost the answer.\n\nBut then again,\n\nWhat do Buldak noodles have to do with fire turkeys?\n\nI suppose we’ll never know.\n\nI’ve forgotten what summer even looks like.\n\nStaring at the heavy snow outside, I can’t help but sigh.\n\nIf I were to leap,\n\nWould it be the Past or the Present that kills me?\n\nThe Past has already happened, and the Present stands idly by.\n\nBy process of elimination, only the Future remains.\n\nBut the Future, armed with a perfect alibi,\n\nStruts around with arrogant impunity.\n\nSo, what kills me\n\nIs the absence of a future.\n\nBut oh, merciful God of Instant Noodles,\n\nI once swore to You—\n\nI would remain forever loyal to Shin Ramyun,\n\nNever reaching my greedy hands toward the Buldak cult.\n\nYet here I am, standing in utter solitude,\n\nPersonally slaughtering my own vow.\n\nJust as I once told my mother\n\nI would never smoke.\n\nNow I realize\n\nThat \"forever\" arrives much sooner than expected.\n\nBut oh, kind God of Instant Noodles,\n\nDo You still remember?\n\nWe once fought side by side,\n\nDespising the disciples of Buldak,\n\nDeeming that filthy cuisine the darkest of heresies.\n\nBut I—but I—\n\nAt some untraceable moment,\n\nStopped feeling Your presence.\n\nI curled up in a field of black rye,\n\nLetting it swallow me whole.\n\nThat was the first time I cried with such stillness.\n\nAre You still there, my Lord?\n\nI choose to believe\n\nThat the snow outside\n\nIs the penance You have cast down.\n\nI kneel in Your boundless white, sobbing, confessing.\n\nBut oh, heartless God of Instant Noodles,\n\nI cannot decipher Your oracles.\n\nI don't understand why You left,\n\nAbandoning me to this bitter winter,\n\nLetting the frost pierce through my ribs.\n\nIn my moment of deepest despair,\n\nIt was the Buldak noodles\n\nThat took me into their embrace.\n\nBuldak noodles are tasty,\n\nBut I don't like Buldak noodles.\n\nYou know this.\n\nBut I had no choice.\n\nBetween eternal darkness and total depravity,\n\nI simply chose to survive.\n\nBut if—just if—\n\nYou ever descend upon this wasteland again,\n\nRemember that field of black rye I told You about.\n\nErect a headstone for me.\n\nOffer a pack of Shin Ramyun,\n\nAnd perhaps a Matcha Latte,\n\nTo mourn me, and Your former glory.12:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-01-03-to-the-god-of-instant-noodles\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-01-03-to-the-god-of-instant-noodles\",\"title\":\"To the God of Instant Noodles\",\"date\":\"2026-01-02T23:49:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"I must have been hallucinating at the time; I should have gone to bed earlier.\",\"content\":\"$1d\"}}]\n1e:T693,The airport is a magical place.\n\nPeople meet and part, sprint and wait. Look—there’s a man with a guitar, adding a silent background track to his own journey. There’s another with a bouquet, glancing around, wondering when his luck will finally land. And someone else, lugging a massive suitcase and buying a neck pillow, perhaps waking up on the other side of the ocean.\n\nFates intertwine, then sever. This is likely the first time you’ll brush shoulders with him, and the last. These fleeting encounters create a peculiar energy field, one that allows me to detach from my \"subject\" self and observe—inward or outward—as an \"object.\"\n\nStep through the gate, put on the eye mask, pick a random loading animation, and let your memories save. Don't fear the turbulence; it’s just the new terrain loading.\n\n\"Could I have a coffee, please?\" I wonder if this flicker of heart palpitations is from the caffeine or the anticipation of seeing a new flame—or an old love. Then I realize: it’s the greedy timeline stretching out, rooting itself like a tree into every corner of the universe. That feeling of weightlessness? That’s me falling into a new abyss of time. I didn't jump; I slipped. How could I ever be prepared, especially when met by that gentle, drowsy voice asking, \"Chicken rice or pasta?\"\n\nMy thorned time capsule rests quietly outside the window. The bold print on its manual—\"PIT 6:00 AM\"—reminds me that this abyss is one I dove into of my own volition.\n\n\"What could be wrong\" has become my mantra lately. I can always find a reason to do something.\n\nSo I wait, then I run. Once I find a reason I truly believe in, I set off.\n\nA new journey begins.13:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2025-12-10-airport-5-30-am\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2025-12-10-airport-5-30-am\",\"title\":\"Airport, 5:30 AM\",\"date\":\"2025-12-09T23:53:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"The airport is a magical place.\",\"content\":\"$1e\"}}]\n1f:T49e,I’m dying. I can feel it.\n\nThe clouds at the horizon reflect the afterglow of the setting sun; autumn in Pittsburgh remains cool and crisp. The brilliant city lights and the roar of machinery make it feel as though this city has caught \"The New York Fever.\"\n\nDoes this world truly have anything to do with me? The heavy traffic, the sunrises and sunsets—it seems the world doesn't care who is missing. After all, this is just a pale blue, lonely dot. All the \"good\" and \"bad\" are merely our own wishful thinking.\n\nThis is truly a strange place. Society is like a giant pendulum, swinging through the void, generating beauty and ugliness, high and low, meaning and purpose.\n\n*Click, click, click, click.* The traffic light counts down. Perhaps this is what dying feels like. Sinking deeper and deeper, I reach out with a thousand hands, searching for a branch to save my life, clutching it tight and praying it doesn't snap too soon.\n\nRock, rap, cigarettes, alcohol, literature, art. These elements and labels are like nails, pinning seven billion people firmly to the cliffside, keeping them from plummeting into the abyss.\n\nI hope you find your own nail on this precipice, too.14:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2025-11-27-on-the-cliffs\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2025-11-27-on-the-cliffs\",\"title\":\"On the Cliffs\",\"date\":\"2025-11-26T23:58:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"I’m dying. I can feel it.\",\"content\":\"$1f\"}}]\n20:T5c3,Heading home. A whole year drifting abroad, yet it feels like a mere blink of an eye. Still, it is just me—shouldering my pack, setting off on the journey back.\n\nThe moment the final exam ended, the cacophony ceased. That instant of silence caught me off guard. Headphones on, an Americano in hand, a cigarette lit. I can’t tell if it’s the nicotine or the caffeine at work, but the world has never looked so bright. The Ferry (渡口) plays in my ears, and for a moment, I can’t distinguish joy from sorrow. Years ago, on another afternoon, I once danced to this very song.\n\nMoments of tranquility are so precious, yet they are like a leaky wooden boat—eventually, they will be filled by new noise and sink. I keep clicking \"Next,\" hoping the music will rescue me from this lonely vessel. Then, I stop. It’s \"Black Butterfly.\" I don't understand why the melody is so tender, but that tenderness leaves me defenseless. I’ve never dared to face such gentleness before. Yes, I’m going home. Going home alone.\n\nBut this time is different. I embrace that tenderness without hesitation. A wave of heat brushes against my cheeks, coaxing a smile. Perhaps I’ve forgiven the world, or the world has forgiven me. I stop walking; behind me lies the fading glow of the sunset.\n\nIt’s getting late. I think it’s time to head back. I take off my headphones and dial her number.\n\nIn this Allegheny blue hour, it is no longer just me, shouldering my pack, heading home.15:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-04-24-the-first-monologue\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-04-24-the-first-monologue\",\"title\":\"The Way Home\",\"date\":\"2025-05-02T08:00:00.000-04:00\",\"excerpt\":\"Thank you. Thank the world.\",\"content\":\"$20\"}}]\n1a:[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"}]]\n21:I[27201,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"IconMark\"]\n18:null\n1c:[[\"$\",\"title\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"Personal Portfolio\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"description\",\"content\":\"Crafting code, capturing light, and making some noise.\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"2\",{\"rel\":\"icon\",\"href\":\"/favicon.ico?favicon.0b3bf435.ico\",\"sizes\":\"256x256\",\"type\":\"image/x-icon\"}],[\"$\",\"$L21\",\"3\",{}]]\n","segmentData":{"/_tree":":HL[\"/_next/static/chunks/330a17ddee87f907.css\",\"style\"]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/media/70bc3e132a0a741e-s.p.15008bfb.woff2\",\"font\",{\"crossOrigin\":\"\",\"type\":\"font/woff2\"}]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/media/83afe278b6a6bb3c-s.p.3a6ba036.woff2\",\"font\",{\"crossOrigin\":\"\",\"type\":\"font/woff2\"}]\n0:{\"buildId\":\"Q5K0UX1dTfp8SZPg-L6Wj\",\"tree\":{\"name\":\"\",\"paramType\":null,\"paramKey\":\"\",\"hasRuntimePrefetch\":false,\"slots\":{\"children\":{\"name\":\"about\",\"paramType\":null,\"paramKey\":\"about\",\"hasRuntimePrefetch\":false,\"slots\":{\"children\":{\"name\":\"__PAGE__\",\"paramType\":null,\"paramKey\":\"__PAGE__\",\"hasRuntimePrefetch\":false,\"slots\":null,\"isRootLayout\":false}},\"isRootLayout\":false}},\"isRootLayout\":true},\"staleTime\":300}\n","/_full":"1:\"$Sreact.fragment\"\n2:I[39756,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"default\"]\n3:I[37457,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"default\"]\n4:I[22016,[\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\"],\"\"]\nd:I[68027,[],\"default\"]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/chunks/330a17ddee87f907.css\",\"style\"]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/media/70bc3e132a0a741e-s.p.15008bfb.woff2\",\"font\",{\"crossOrigin\":\"\",\"type\":\"font/woff2\"}]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/media/83afe278b6a6bb3c-s.p.3a6ba036.woff2\",\"font\",{\"crossOrigin\":\"\",\"type\":\"font/woff2\"}]\n0:{\"P\":null,\"b\":\"Q5K0UX1dTfp8SZPg-L6Wj\",\"c\":[\"\",\"about\"],\"q\":\"\",\"i\":false,\"f\":[[[\"\",{\"children\":[\"about\",{\"children\":[\"__PAGE__\",{}]}]},\"$undefined\",\"$undefined\",true],[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[[[\"$\",\"link\",\"0\",{\"rel\":\"stylesheet\",\"href\":\"/_next/static/chunks/330a17ddee87f907.css\",\"precedence\":\"next\",\"crossOrigin\":\"$undefined\",\"nonce\":\"$undefined\"}]],[\"$\",\"html\",null,{\"lang\":\"en\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"body\",null,{\"className\":\"inter_5901b7c6-module__ec5Qua__variable jetbrains_mono_8be3b53a-module__CxiPNW__variable antialiased relative min-h-screen overflow-x-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fixed inset-0 z-50 pointer-events-none grainy opacity-20\"}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"className\":\"relative z-10 min-h-screen flex flex-col\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"error\":\"$undefined\",\"errorStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"errorScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{}],\"templateStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"templateScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"notFound\":[[[\"$\",\"title\",null,{\"children\":\"404: This page could not be found.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"fontFamily\":\"system-ui,\\\"Segoe UI\\\",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,\\\"Apple Color Emoji\\\",\\\"Segoe UI Emoji\\\"\",\"height\":\"100vh\",\"textAlign\":\"center\",\"display\":\"flex\",\"flexDirection\":\"column\",\"alignItems\":\"center\",\"justifyContent\":\"center\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"style\",null,{\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"body{color:#000;background:#fff;margin:0}.next-error-h1{border-right:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.3)}@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#fff;background:#000}.next-error-h1{border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.3)}}\"}}],[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"next-error-h1\",\"style\":{\"display\":\"inline-block\",\"margin\":\"0 20px 0 0\",\"padding\":\"0 23px 0 0\",\"fontSize\":24,\"fontWeight\":500,\"verticalAlign\":\"top\",\"lineHeight\":\"49px\"},\"children\":404}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"display\":\"inline-block\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"style\":{\"fontSize\":14,\"fontWeight\":400,\"lineHeight\":\"49px\",\"margin\":0},\"children\":\"This page could not be found.\"}]}]]}]}]],[]],\"forbidden\":\"$undefined\",\"unauthorized\":\"$undefined\"}]}]]}]}]]}],{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"error\":\"$undefined\",\"errorStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"errorScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{}],\"templateStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"templateScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"notFound\":\"$undefined\",\"forbidden\":\"$undefined\",\"unauthorized\":\"$undefined\"}]]}],{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"min-h-screen bg-[#060606] text-neutral-400 font-light selection:bg-white selection:text-black pb-32\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"header\",null,{\"className\":\"fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-40 p-6 grid grid-cols-3 items-center bg-[#060606]/80 backdrop-blur-sm border-b border-neutral-900/50\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-start\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L4\",null,{\"href\":\"/\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"group relative inline-flex items-center justify-center px-4 py-2 font-sans transition-colors duration-300 text-sm text-neutral-500 hover:text-white\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mr-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"[\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"tracking-widest uppercase\",\"children\":\"< Back\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ml-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"]\"}]]}]}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-center\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm md:text-base font-normal tracking-[0.3em] text-neutral-300 uppercase\",\"children\":\"About\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-end\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L4\",null,{\"href\":\"/photos/uploads/resume_tech_eng.pdf\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"group relative inline-flex items-center justify-center px-4 py-2 font-sans transition-colors duration-300 text-sm text-neutral-500 hover:text-white\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mr-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"[\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"tracking-widest uppercase\",\"children\":\"Resume\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ml-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"]\"}]]}]}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"className\":\"pt-48 px-6 md:px-12 max-w-[1800px] mx-auto\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-32 border-b border-neutral-800 pb-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-4xl md:text-5xl font-light tracking-tight text-neutral-200 mb-6 uppercase\",\"children\":\"Cheerful Nonsense.\"}],\"$L5\",\"$L6\"]}],\"$L7\",\"$L8\"]}]]}],[\"$L9\",\"$La\"],\"$Lb\"]}],{},null,false,false]},null,false,false]},null,false,false],\"$Lc\",false]],\"m\":\"$undefined\",\"G\":[\"$d\",[]],\"S\":true}\nf:I[78588,[\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\"],\"JournalCard\"]\n16:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"OutletBoundary\"]\n17:\"$Sreact.suspense\"\n19:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"ViewportBoundary\"]\n1b:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"MetadataBoundary\"]\n5:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-lg text-neutral-500 font-mono tracking-tighter mb-8\",\"children\":\"> My monologue.\"}]\ne:T5d1,Hi. I'm Zuocheng Wang.  I'm currently in University of Pittsburgh undergraduate program, majoring 'Computer Science' and 'Digital Narrative & Interactive Design'. I’d like to think of myself as someone with a broad range of interests. If you walked up to me and started talking about your hobbies, no matter what they are, I could probably hold my own in the conversation. \n\nI’ve always had a bone to pick with the Chinese public education system’s obsession with sorting people into \"Arts vs. Sciences.\" It creates this false dichotomy, subtly erasing the possibility of being both. To me, art decides the destination, while technology decides how to get there; possessing both is the truly rare skill of our time. This isn't \"distraction\" or \"unproductive.\" My enjoyment is my greatest priority.\n\nAs the headline says, \"The world is a cheerful nonsense\" (Hermann Hesse). There is no singular, noble \"meaning\" out there that demands we sacrifice infinite possibilities in our short lives. Look, the world is getting more beautiful by the second.\n\nThis page was originally intended to be a traditional CV. However, since the cool stuffs I made are already documented in their respective sections, and my formal academic information and technical history can be found in my resume linked in the corner, a standard CV felt redundant here. Instead, I've converted this space into a living archive, a repository for monologues, fragments, and late-night thoughts. Welcome to the journal.6:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"text-neutral-400 text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap\",\"children\":[\"$e\"]}]\n10:T5ca,My mom asked me why I suddenly decided not to start working right after graduation.I told her:\n\n“Why not? I love the feeling of having options.I want to get into Big Tech—the money is great. But then again, maybe not—it’s exhausting, and I’d probably lose my hair to the grind. I want to be a Product Manager; the sense of achievement from building something is huge. But then again, maybe not—dealing with a hundred different personalities and playing the peacemaker sounds like a drag.\n\nI want to make films, maybe join a top-tier production house like MediaStorm. I want to be a creator, like the guys at Speedster. I even want to build rockets from scratch in a backyard workshop.\n\nI want to be a photographer, join a film crew, drive race cars for a factory team, or be a designer. I want to make music—play guitar in a dive bar and sing whatever song I’m obsessed with lately, not giving a damn whether the crowd is listening or not.\n\nI just suddenly realized: if I finish a Master’s degree and just start climbing the corporate ladder, my life is basically set in stone. Sure, I’ll make plenty of money and settle down in a big city, but I’ll lose my choices. The cost of turning back will be too high. Kids need school, and a wife might be shouting about divorce every time she’s unhappy. Good God!\n\nI just want to grab it all while I’m young: the best views, the best girls, the best version of myself, and the best opportunities.”\n\nMy Mom: 😮11:T9e7,\"I miss you. What are you up to?\"\n\nI don't know who to send this to. I’ll just leave it here.\n\nHave I become the person I once despised, living off hollow intimacy? I’ve grown used to having someone—beside me or in my phone. It doesn't really matter who. I just need that silent belief that someone loves me, that someone \"got my back.\" Eventually, I grow tired, annoyed. I watch her cry until her tears run dry, then I replace her with someone new, feeling absolutely nothing. All those blurred lines and ambiguities... perhaps they are just the shadows of \"youth.\" Dark, yet structured, they are the evidence of my existence on the canvas—the inevitable result of being exposed to the sun. Or so I tell myself to ease the guilt.\n\nI used to be so insecure. I witnessed couples blooming all around me; I watched my friends walk away and into the arms of others, leaving me behind. I blessed them from the bottom of my heart, yet I screamed from the depths of it, too. I craved validation so much. I was so desperate for tenderness. I just wanted someone to hold me and tell me, \"Everything is going to be okay.\" To treat me like a child, or an infant. If I could return to the womb, that would be even better. Maybe my mother had her own troubles back then, or perhaps she simply lacked the power to stuff me back in and start over.\n\nIs being liked the only form of self-affirmation? I refuse to believe that. So, I slapped my younger self across the face. He became even more insecure.\n\nFor someone with such fierce pride, I never resisted dressing as a girl. Staring at that stranger in the mirror, I’d sigh—that kind of girl would never love me, but I could briefly become her. But when I take off the wig and the skirt, she doesn’t leave. She made me fall in love with a concept, an image, a perfect \"Me.\" So maybe, there was never room for anyone else in my heart. There is only me, and Me.\n\n\"What are we now?\" I have no answer. Am I afraid of a predetermined future? Promises I can't keep? Or is it that I simply don't love you as much as I love myself? Of course I love you, but I just love everyone equally... right? Or do I just want to be loved by everyone equally, so I can squander it as I please.\n\nThrough all these years of drifting, the only skill I’ve truly mastered is \"not giving a damn.\" I never learned how to meet or embrace; I only learned how to say goodbye. I know I’m pushing a boulder. I know it will eventually roll back down. I know this is my punishment.\n\nTell me, Mr. Camus—am I happy?7:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-16 mb-64\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-02-24-the-future-is-yet-to-come\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-02-24-the-future-is-yet-to-come\",\"title\":\"The future is yet to come\",\"date\":\"2026-02-24T00:03:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"Don't want to limit myself.\",\"content\":\"$10\"}}],[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-02-16-exhausted\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-02-16-exhausted\",\"title\":\"Exhausted\",\"date\":\"2026-02-16T00:00:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"\\\"I miss you. What are you up to?\\\"\",\"content\":\"$11\"}}],\"$L12\",\"$L13\",\"$L14\",\"$L15\"]}]\n8:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto text-left\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-neutral-600 font-mono\",\"children\":[\"//\",\" Thinking in public.\"]}]}]\n9:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-0\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"async\":true,\"nonce\":\"$undefined\"}]\na:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-1\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\",\"async\":true,\"nonce\":\"$undefined\"}]\nb:[\"$\",\"$L16\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$17\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.MetadataOutlet\",\"children\":\"$@18\"}]}]\nc:[\"$\",\"$1\",\"h\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L19\",null,{\"children\":\"$L1a\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"hidden\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L1b\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$17\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.Metadata\",\"children\":\"$L1c\"}]}]}],[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"next-size-adjust\",\"content\":\"\"}]]}]\n1d:T917,I just whipped up a bowl of Buldak noodles,\n\nWhen a friend sent over a photo—\n\nA plate of crawfish we once shared.\n\nWhat defines a \"crawfish\" now?\n\nI’ve long lost the answer.\n\nBut then again,\n\nWhat do Buldak noodles have to do with fire turkeys?\n\nI suppose we’ll never know.\n\nI’ve forgotten what summer even looks like.\n\nStaring at the heavy snow outside, I can’t help but sigh.\n\nIf I were to leap,\n\nWould it be the Past or the Present that kills me?\n\nThe Past has already happened, and the Present stands idly by.\n\nBy process of elimination, only the Future remains.\n\nBut the Future, armed with a perfect alibi,\n\nStruts around with arrogant impunity.\n\nSo, what kills me\n\nIs the absence of a future.\n\nBut oh, merciful God of Instant Noodles,\n\nI once swore to You—\n\nI would remain forever loyal to Shin Ramyun,\n\nNever reaching my greedy hands toward the Buldak cult.\n\nYet here I am, standing in utter solitude,\n\nPersonally slaughtering my own vow.\n\nJust as I once told my mother\n\nI would never smoke.\n\nNow I realize\n\nThat \"forever\" arrives much sooner than expected.\n\nBut oh, kind God of Instant Noodles,\n\nDo You still remember?\n\nWe once fought side by side,\n\nDespising the disciples of Buldak,\n\nDeeming that filthy cuisine the darkest of heresies.\n\nBut I—but I—\n\nAt some untraceable moment,\n\nStopped feeling Your presence.\n\nI curled up in a field of black rye,\n\nLetting it swallow me whole.\n\nThat was the first time I cried with such stillness.\n\nAre You still there, my Lord?\n\nI choose to believe\n\nThat the snow outside\n\nIs the penance You have cast down.\n\nI kneel in Your boundless white, sobbing, confessing.\n\nBut oh, heartless God of Instant Noodles,\n\nI cannot decipher Your oracles.\n\nI don't understand why You left,\n\nAbandoning me to this bitter winter,\n\nLetting the frost pierce through my ribs.\n\nIn my moment of deepest despair,\n\nIt was the Buldak noodles\n\nThat took me into their embrace.\n\nBuldak noodles are tasty,\n\nBut I don't like Buldak noodles.\n\nYou know this.\n\nBut I had no choice.\n\nBetween eternal darkness and total depravity,\n\nI simply chose to survive.\n\nBut if—just if—\n\nYou ever descend upon this wasteland again,\n\nRemember that field of black rye I told You about.\n\nErect a headstone for me.\n\nOffer a pack of Shin Ramyun,\n\nAnd perhaps a Matcha Latte,\n\nTo mourn me, and Your former glory.12:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-01-03-to-the-god-of-instant-noodles\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-01-03-to-the-god-of-instant-noodles\",\"title\":\"To the God of Instant Noodles\",\"date\":\"2026-01-02T23:49:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"I must have been hallucinating at the time; I should have gone to bed earlier.\",\"content\":\"$1d\"}}]\n1e:T693,The airport is a magical place.\n\nPeople meet and part, sprint and wait. Look—there’s a man with a guitar, adding a silent background track to his own journey. There’s another with a bouquet, glancing around, wondering when his luck will finally land. And someone else, lugging a massive suitcase and buying a neck pillow, perhaps waking up on the other side of the ocean.\n\nFates intertwine, then sever. This is likely the first time you’ll brush shoulders with him, and the last. These fleeting encounters create a peculiar energy field, one that allows me to detach from my \"subject\" self and observe—inward or outward—as an \"object.\"\n\nStep through the gate, put on the eye mask, pick a random loading animation, and let your memories save. Don't fear the turbulence; it’s just the new terrain loading.\n\n\"Could I have a coffee, please?\" I wonder if this flicker of heart palpitations is from the caffeine or the anticipation of seeing a new flame—or an old love. Then I realize: it’s the greedy timeline stretching out, rooting itself like a tree into every corner of the universe. That feeling of weightlessness? That’s me falling into a new abyss of time. I didn't jump; I slipped. How could I ever be prepared, especially when met by that gentle, drowsy voice asking, \"Chicken rice or pasta?\"\n\nMy thorned time capsule rests quietly outside the window. The bold print on its manual—\"PIT 6:00 AM\"—reminds me that this abyss is one I dove into of my own volition.\n\n\"What could be wrong\" has become my mantra lately. I can always find a reason to do something.\n\nSo I wait, then I run. Once I find a reason I truly believe in, I set off.\n\nA new journey begins.13:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2025-12-10-airport-5-30-am\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2025-12-10-airport-5-30-am\",\"title\":\"Airport, 5:30 AM\",\"date\":\"2025-12-09T23:53:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"The airport is a magical place.\",\"content\":\"$1e\"}}]\n1f:T49e,I’m dying. I can feel it.\n\nThe clouds at the horizon reflect the afterglow of the setting sun; autumn in Pittsburgh remains cool and crisp. The brilliant city lights and the roar of machinery make it feel as though this city has caught \"The New York Fever.\"\n\nDoes this world truly have anything to do with me? The heavy traffic, the sunrises and sunsets—it seems the world doesn't care who is missing. After all, this is just a pale blue, lonely dot. All the \"good\" and \"bad\" are merely our own wishful thinking.\n\nThis is truly a strange place. Society is like a giant pendulum, swinging through the void, generating beauty and ugliness, high and low, meaning and purpose.\n\n*Click, click, click, click.* The traffic light counts down. Perhaps this is what dying feels like. Sinking deeper and deeper, I reach out with a thousand hands, searching for a branch to save my life, clutching it tight and praying it doesn't snap too soon.\n\nRock, rap, cigarettes, alcohol, literature, art. These elements and labels are like nails, pinning seven billion people firmly to the cliffside, keeping them from plummeting into the abyss.\n\nI hope you find your own nail on this precipice, too.14:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2025-11-27-on-the-cliffs\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2025-11-27-on-the-cliffs\",\"title\":\"On the Cliffs\",\"date\":\"2025-11-26T23:58:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"I’m dying. I can feel it.\",\"content\":\"$1f\"}}]\n20:T5c3,Heading home. A whole year drifting abroad, yet it feels like a mere blink of an eye. Still, it is just me—shouldering my pack, setting off on the journey back.\n\nThe moment the final exam ended, the cacophony ceased. That instant of silence caught me off guard. Headphones on, an Americano in hand, a cigarette lit. I can’t tell if it’s the nicotine or the caffeine at work, but the world has never looked so bright. The Ferry (渡口) plays in my ears, and for a moment, I can’t distinguish joy from sorrow. Years ago, on another afternoon, I once danced to this very song.\n\nMoments of tranquility are so precious, yet they are like a leaky wooden boat—eventually, they will be filled by new noise and sink. I keep clicking \"Next,\" hoping the music will rescue me from this lonely vessel. Then, I stop. It’s \"Black Butterfly.\" I don't understand why the melody is so tender, but that tenderness leaves me defenseless. I’ve never dared to face such gentleness before. Yes, I’m going home. Going home alone.\n\nBut this time is different. I embrace that tenderness without hesitation. A wave of heat brushes against my cheeks, coaxing a smile. Perhaps I’ve forgiven the world, or the world has forgiven me. I stop walking; behind me lies the fading glow of the sunset.\n\nIt’s getting late. I think it’s time to head back. I take off my headphones and dial her number.\n\nIn this Allegheny blue hour, it is no longer just me, shouldering my pack, heading home.15:[\"$\",\"$Lf\",\"2026-04-24-the-first-monologue\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-04-24-the-first-monologue\",\"title\":\"The Way Home\",\"date\":\"2025-05-02T08:00:00.000-04:00\",\"excerpt\":\"Thank you. Thank the world.\",\"content\":\"$20\"}}]\n1a:[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"}]]\n21:I[27201,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"IconMark\"]\n18:null\n1c:[[\"$\",\"title\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"Personal Portfolio\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"description\",\"content\":\"Crafting code, capturing light, and making some noise.\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"2\",{\"rel\":\"icon\",\"href\":\"/favicon.ico?favicon.0b3bf435.ico\",\"sizes\":\"256x256\",\"type\":\"image/x-icon\"}],[\"$\",\"$L21\",\"3\",{}]]\n","/about/__PAGE__":"1:\"$Sreact.fragment\"\n2:I[22016,[\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\"],\"\"]\n9:I[78588,[\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\"],\"JournalCard\"]\n10:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"OutletBoundary\"]\n11:\"$Sreact.suspense\"\n3:T5d1,Hi. I'm Zuocheng Wang.  I'm currently in University of Pittsburgh undergraduate program, majoring 'Computer Science' and 'Digital Narrative & Interactive Design'. I’d like to think of myself as someone with a broad range of interests. If you walked up to me and started talking about your hobbies, no matter what they are, I could probably hold my own in the conversation. \n\nI’ve always had a bone to pick with the Chinese public education system’s obsession with sorting people into \"Arts vs. Sciences.\" It creates this false dichotomy, subtly erasing the possibility of being both. To me, art decides the destination, while technology decides how to get there; possessing both is the truly rare skill of our time. This isn't \"distraction\" or \"unproductive.\" My enjoyment is my greatest priority.\n\nAs the headline says, \"The world is a cheerful nonsense\" (Hermann Hesse). There is no singular, noble \"meaning\" out there that demands we sacrifice infinite possibilities in our short lives. Look, the world is getting more beautiful by the second.\n\nThis page was originally intended to be a traditional CV. However, since the cool stuffs I made are already documented in their respective sections, and my formal academic information and technical history can be found in my resume linked in the corner, a standard CV felt redundant here. Instead, I've converted this space into a living archive, a repository for monologues, fragments, and late-night thoughts. Welcome to the journal.0:{\"buildId\":\"Q5K0UX1dTfp8SZPg-L6Wj\",\"rsc\":[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"min-h-screen bg-[#060606] text-neutral-400 font-light selection:bg-white selection:text-black pb-32\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"header\",null,{\"className\":\"fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-40 p-6 grid grid-cols-3 items-center bg-[#060606]/80 backdrop-blur-sm border-b border-neutral-900/50\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-start\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"href\":\"/\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"group relative inline-flex items-center justify-center px-4 py-2 font-sans transition-colors duration-300 text-sm text-neutral-500 hover:text-white\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mr-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"[\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"tracking-widest uppercase\",\"children\":\"< Back\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ml-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"]\"}]]}]}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-center\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm md:text-base font-normal tracking-[0.3em] text-neutral-300 uppercase\",\"children\":\"About\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex justify-end\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"href\":\"/photos/uploads/resume_tech_eng.pdf\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"group relative inline-flex items-center justify-center px-4 py-2 font-sans transition-colors duration-300 text-sm text-neutral-500 hover:text-white\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mr-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"[\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"tracking-widest uppercase\",\"children\":\"Resume\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ml-2 opacity-50 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity\",\"children\":\"]\"}]]}]}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"className\":\"pt-48 px-6 md:px-12 max-w-[1800px] mx-auto\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-32 border-b border-neutral-800 pb-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-4xl md:text-5xl font-light tracking-tight text-neutral-200 mb-6 uppercase\",\"children\":\"Cheerful Nonsense.\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-lg text-neutral-500 font-mono tracking-tighter mb-8\",\"children\":\"> My monologue.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"text-neutral-400 text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap\",\"children\":[\"$3\"]}]]}],\"$L4\",\"$L5\"]}]]}],[\"$L6\",\"$L7\"],\"$L8\"]}],\"loading\":null,\"isPartial\":false}\na:T5ca,My mom asked me why I suddenly decided not to start working right after graduation.I told her:\n\n“Why not? I love the feeling of having options.I want to get into Big Tech—the money is great. But then again, maybe not—it’s exhausting, and I’d probably lose my hair to the grind. I want to be a Product Manager; the sense of achievement from building something is huge. But then again, maybe not—dealing with a hundred different personalities and playing the peacemaker sounds like a drag.\n\nI want to make films, maybe join a top-tier production house like MediaStorm. I want to be a creator, like the guys at Speedster. I even want to build rockets from scratch in a backyard workshop.\n\nI want to be a photographer, join a film crew, drive race cars for a factory team, or be a designer. I want to make music—play guitar in a dive bar and sing whatever song I’m obsessed with lately, not giving a damn whether the crowd is listening or not.\n\nI just suddenly realized: if I finish a Master’s degree and just start climbing the corporate ladder, my life is basically set in stone. Sure, I’ll make plenty of money and settle down in a big city, but I’ll lose my choices. The cost of turning back will be too high. Kids need school, and a wife might be shouting about divorce every time she’s unhappy. Good God!\n\nI just want to grab it all while I’m young: the best views, the best girls, the best version of myself, and the best opportunities.”\n\nMy Mom: 😮b:T9e7,\"I miss you. What are you up to?\"\n\nI don't know who to send this to. I’ll just leave it here.\n\nHave I become the person I once despised, living off hollow intimacy? I’ve grown used to having someone—beside me or in my phone. It doesn't really matter who. I just need that silent belief that someone loves me, that someone \"got my back.\" Eventually, I grow tired, annoyed. I watch her cry until her tears run dry, then I replace her with someone new, feeling absolutely nothing. All those blurred lines and ambiguities... perhaps they are just the shadows of \"youth.\" Dark, yet structured, they are the evidence of my existence on the canvas—the inevitable result of being exposed to the sun. Or so I tell myself to ease the guilt.\n\nI used to be so insecure. I witnessed couples blooming all around me; I watched my friends walk away and into the arms of others, leaving me behind. I blessed them from the bottom of my heart, yet I screamed from the depths of it, too. I craved validation so much. I was so desperate for tenderness. I just wanted someone to hold me and tell me, \"Everything is going to be okay.\" To treat me like a child, or an infant. If I could return to the womb, that would be even better. Maybe my mother had her own troubles back then, or perhaps she simply lacked the power to stuff me back in and start over.\n\nIs being liked the only form of self-affirmation? I refuse to believe that. So, I slapped my younger self across the face. He became even more insecure.\n\nFor someone with such fierce pride, I never resisted dressing as a girl. Staring at that stranger in the mirror, I’d sigh—that kind of girl would never love me, but I could briefly become her. But when I take off the wig and the skirt, she doesn’t leave. She made me fall in love with a concept, an image, a perfect \"Me.\" So maybe, there was never room for anyone else in my heart. There is only me, and Me.\n\n\"What are we now?\" I have no answer. Am I afraid of a predetermined future? Promises I can't keep? Or is it that I simply don't love you as much as I love myself? Of course I love you, but I just love everyone equally... right? Or do I just want to be loved by everyone equally, so I can squander it as I please.\n\nThrough all these years of drifting, the only skill I’ve truly mastered is \"not giving a damn.\" I never learned how to meet or embrace; I only learned how to say goodbye. I know I’m pushing a boulder. I know it will eventually roll back down. I know this is my punishment.\n\nTell me, Mr. Camus—am I happy?4:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-16 mb-64\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L9\",\"2026-02-24-the-future-is-yet-to-come\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-02-24-the-future-is-yet-to-come\",\"title\":\"The future is yet to come\",\"date\":\"2026-02-24T00:03:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"Don't want to limit myself.\",\"content\":\"$a\"}}],[\"$\",\"$L9\",\"2026-02-16-exhausted\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-02-16-exhausted\",\"title\":\"Exhausted\",\"date\":\"2026-02-16T00:00:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"\\\"I miss you. What are you up to?\\\"\",\"content\":\"$b\"}}],\"$Lc\",\"$Ld\",\"$Le\",\"$Lf\"]}]\n5:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-4xl mx-auto text-left\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-neutral-600 font-mono\",\"children\":[\"//\",\" Thinking in public.\"]}]}]\n6:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-0\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/4b421af3782959a9.js\",\"async\":true}]\n7:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-1\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/9006a9dbed1389eb.js\",\"async\":true}]\n8:[\"$\",\"$L10\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$11\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.MetadataOutlet\",\"children\":\"$@12\"}]}]\n13:T917,I just whipped up a bowl of Buldak noodles,\n\nWhen a friend sent over a photo—\n\nA plate of crawfish we once shared.\n\nWhat defines a \"crawfish\" now?\n\nI’ve long lost the answer.\n\nBut then again,\n\nWhat do Buldak noodles have to do with fire turkeys?\n\nI suppose we’ll never know.\n\nI’ve forgotten what summer even looks like.\n\nStaring at the heavy snow outside, I can’t help but sigh.\n\nIf I were to leap,\n\nWould it be the Past or the Present that kills me?\n\nThe Past has already happened, and the Present stands idly by.\n\nBy process of elimination, only the Future remains.\n\nBut the Future, armed with a perfect alibi,\n\nStruts around with arrogant impunity.\n\nSo, what kills me\n\nIs the absence of a future.\n\nBut oh, merciful God of Instant Noodles,\n\nI once swore to You—\n\nI would remain forever loyal to Shin Ramyun,\n\nNever reaching my greedy hands toward the Buldak cult.\n\nYet here I am, standing in utter solitude,\n\nPersonally slaughtering my own vow.\n\nJust as I once told my mother\n\nI would never smoke.\n\nNow I realize\n\nThat \"forever\" arrives much sooner than expected.\n\nBut oh, kind God of Instant Noodles,\n\nDo You still remember?\n\nWe once fought side by side,\n\nDespising the disciples of Buldak,\n\nDeeming that filthy cuisine the darkest of heresies.\n\nBut I—but I—\n\nAt some untraceable moment,\n\nStopped feeling Your presence.\n\nI curled up in a field of black rye,\n\nLetting it swallow me whole.\n\nThat was the first time I cried with such stillness.\n\nAre You still there, my Lord?\n\nI choose to believe\n\nThat the snow outside\n\nIs the penance You have cast down.\n\nI kneel in Your boundless white, sobbing, confessing.\n\nBut oh, heartless God of Instant Noodles,\n\nI cannot decipher Your oracles.\n\nI don't understand why You left,\n\nAbandoning me to this bitter winter,\n\nLetting the frost pierce through my ribs.\n\nIn my moment of deepest despair,\n\nIt was the Buldak noodles\n\nThat took me into their embrace.\n\nBuldak noodles are tasty,\n\nBut I don't like Buldak noodles.\n\nYou know this.\n\nBut I had no choice.\n\nBetween eternal darkness and total depravity,\n\nI simply chose to survive.\n\nBut if—just if—\n\nYou ever descend upon this wasteland again,\n\nRemember that field of black rye I told You about.\n\nErect a headstone for me.\n\nOffer a pack of Shin Ramyun,\n\nAnd perhaps a Matcha Latte,\n\nTo mourn me, and Your former glory.c:[\"$\",\"$L9\",\"2026-01-03-to-the-god-of-instant-noodles\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-01-03-to-the-god-of-instant-noodles\",\"title\":\"To the God of Instant Noodles\",\"date\":\"2026-01-02T23:49:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"I must have been hallucinating at the time; I should have gone to bed earlier.\",\"content\":\"$13\"}}]\n14:T693,The airport is a magical place.\n\nPeople meet and part, sprint and wait. Look—there’s a man with a guitar, adding a silent background track to his own journey. There’s another with a bouquet, glancing around, wondering when his luck will finally land. And someone else, lugging a massive suitcase and buying a neck pillow, perhaps waking up on the other side of the ocean.\n\nFates intertwine, then sever. This is likely the first time you’ll brush shoulders with him, and the last. These fleeting encounters create a peculiar energy field, one that allows me to detach from my \"subject\" self and observe—inward or outward—as an \"object.\"\n\nStep through the gate, put on the eye mask, pick a random loading animation, and let your memories save. Don't fear the turbulence; it’s just the new terrain loading.\n\n\"Could I have a coffee, please?\" I wonder if this flicker of heart palpitations is from the caffeine or the anticipation of seeing a new flame—or an old love. Then I realize: it’s the greedy timeline stretching out, rooting itself like a tree into every corner of the universe. That feeling of weightlessness? That’s me falling into a new abyss of time. I didn't jump; I slipped. How could I ever be prepared, especially when met by that gentle, drowsy voice asking, \"Chicken rice or pasta?\"\n\nMy thorned time capsule rests quietly outside the window. The bold print on its manual—\"PIT 6:00 AM\"—reminds me that this abyss is one I dove into of my own volition.\n\n\"What could be wrong\" has become my mantra lately. I can always find a reason to do something.\n\nSo I wait, then I run. Once I find a reason I truly believe in, I set off.\n\nA new journey begins.d:[\"$\",\"$L9\",\"2025-12-10-airport-5-30-am\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2025-12-10-airport-5-30-am\",\"title\":\"Airport, 5:30 AM\",\"date\":\"2025-12-09T23:53:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"The airport is a magical place.\",\"content\":\"$14\"}}]\n15:T49e,I’m dying. I can feel it.\n\nThe clouds at the horizon reflect the afterglow of the setting sun; autumn in Pittsburgh remains cool and crisp. The brilliant city lights and the roar of machinery make it feel as though this city has caught \"The New York Fever.\"\n\nDoes this world truly have anything to do with me? The heavy traffic, the sunrises and sunsets—it seems the world doesn't care who is missing. After all, this is just a pale blue, lonely dot. All the \"good\" and \"bad\" are merely our own wishful thinking.\n\nThis is truly a strange place. Society is like a giant pendulum, swinging through the void, generating beauty and ugliness, high and low, meaning and purpose.\n\n*Click, click, click, click.* The traffic light counts down. Perhaps this is what dying feels like. Sinking deeper and deeper, I reach out with a thousand hands, searching for a branch to save my life, clutching it tight and praying it doesn't snap too soon.\n\nRock, rap, cigarettes, alcohol, literature, art. These elements and labels are like nails, pinning seven billion people firmly to the cliffside, keeping them from plummeting into the abyss.\n\nI hope you find your own nail on this precipice, too.e:[\"$\",\"$L9\",\"2025-11-27-on-the-cliffs\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2025-11-27-on-the-cliffs\",\"title\":\"On the Cliffs\",\"date\":\"2025-11-26T23:58:00.000-05:00\",\"excerpt\":\"I’m dying. I can feel it.\",\"content\":\"$15\"}}]\n16:T5c3,Heading home. A whole year drifting abroad, yet it feels like a mere blink of an eye. Still, it is just me—shouldering my pack, setting off on the journey back.\n\nThe moment the final exam ended, the cacophony ceased. That instant of silence caught me off guard. Headphones on, an Americano in hand, a cigarette lit. I can’t tell if it’s the nicotine or the caffeine at work, but the world has never looked so bright. The Ferry (渡口) plays in my ears, and for a moment, I can’t distinguish joy from sorrow. Years ago, on another afternoon, I once danced to this very song.\n\nMoments of tranquility are so precious, yet they are like a leaky wooden boat—eventually, they will be filled by new noise and sink. I keep clicking \"Next,\" hoping the music will rescue me from this lonely vessel. Then, I stop. It’s \"Black Butterfly.\" I don't understand why the melody is so tender, but that tenderness leaves me defenseless. I’ve never dared to face such gentleness before. Yes, I’m going home. Going home alone.\n\nBut this time is different. I embrace that tenderness without hesitation. A wave of heat brushes against my cheeks, coaxing a smile. Perhaps I’ve forgiven the world, or the world has forgiven me. I stop walking; behind me lies the fading glow of the sunset.\n\nIt’s getting late. I think it’s time to head back. I take off my headphones and dial her number.\n\nIn this Allegheny blue hour, it is no longer just me, shouldering my pack, heading home.f:[\"$\",\"$L9\",\"2026-04-24-the-first-monologue\",{\"entry\":{\"id\":\"2026-04-24-the-first-monologue\",\"title\":\"The Way Home\",\"date\":\"2025-05-02T08:00:00.000-04:00\",\"excerpt\":\"Thank you. Thank the world.\",\"content\":\"$16\"}}]\n12:null\n","/about":"1:\"$Sreact.fragment\"\n2:I[39756,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"default\"]\n3:I[37457,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"default\"]\n0:{\"buildId\":\"Q5K0UX1dTfp8SZPg-L6Wj\",\"rsc\":[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{}]}]]}],\"loading\":null,\"isPartial\":false}\n","/_index":"1:\"$Sreact.fragment\"\n2:I[39756,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"default\"]\n3:I[37457,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"default\"]\n:HL[\"/_next/static/chunks/330a17ddee87f907.css\",\"style\"]\n0:{\"buildId\":\"Q5K0UX1dTfp8SZPg-L6Wj\",\"rsc\":[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[[[\"$\",\"link\",\"0\",{\"rel\":\"stylesheet\",\"href\":\"/_next/static/chunks/330a17ddee87f907.css\",\"precedence\":\"next\"}]],[\"$\",\"html\",null,{\"lang\":\"en\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"body\",null,{\"className\":\"inter_5901b7c6-module__ec5Qua__variable jetbrains_mono_8be3b53a-module__CxiPNW__variable antialiased relative min-h-screen overflow-x-hidden\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fixed inset-0 z-50 pointer-events-none grainy opacity-20\"}],[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"className\":\"relative z-10 min-h-screen flex flex-col\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{}],\"notFound\":[[[\"$\",\"title\",null,{\"children\":\"404: This page could not be found.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"fontFamily\":\"system-ui,\\\"Segoe UI\\\",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,\\\"Apple Color Emoji\\\",\\\"Segoe UI Emoji\\\"\",\"height\":\"100vh\",\"textAlign\":\"center\",\"display\":\"flex\",\"flexDirection\":\"column\",\"alignItems\":\"center\",\"justifyContent\":\"center\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"style\",null,{\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"body{color:#000;background:#fff;margin:0}.next-error-h1{border-right:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.3)}@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#fff;background:#000}.next-error-h1{border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.3)}}\"}}],[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"next-error-h1\",\"style\":{\"display\":\"inline-block\",\"margin\":\"0 20px 0 0\",\"padding\":\"0 23px 0 0\",\"fontSize\":24,\"fontWeight\":500,\"verticalAlign\":\"top\",\"lineHeight\":\"49px\"},\"children\":404}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"display\":\"inline-block\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"style\":{\"fontSize\":14,\"fontWeight\":400,\"lineHeight\":\"49px\",\"margin\":0},\"children\":\"This page could not be found.\"}]}]]}]}]],[]]}]}]]}]}]]}],\"loading\":null,\"isPartial\":false}\n","/_head":"1:\"$Sreact.fragment\"\n2:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"ViewportBoundary\"]\n3:I[97367,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"MetadataBoundary\"]\n4:\"$Sreact.suspense\"\n5:I[27201,[\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d2be314c3ece3fbe.js\"],\"IconMark\"]\n0:{\"buildId\":\"Q5K0UX1dTfp8SZPg-L6Wj\",\"rsc\":[\"$\",\"$1\",\"h\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L2\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"hidden\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$4\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.Metadata\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"title\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"Personal Portfolio\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"description\",\"content\":\"Crafting code, capturing light, and making some noise.\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"2\",{\"rel\":\"icon\",\"href\":\"/favicon.ico?favicon.0b3bf435.ico\",\"sizes\":\"256x256\",\"type\":\"image/x-icon\"}],[\"$\",\"$L5\",\"3\",{}]]}]}]}],[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"next-size-adjust\",\"content\":\"\"}]]}],\"loading\":null,\"isPartial\":false}\n"}}